OBDLink SX combined SAE PID (PID stuffing) probe — 2026-10-02

Basis: OBDwiz 4.36.0 is installed. Its release notes say version 4.30.0 added
“Combine SAE PID Requests” (PID stuffing), which can greatly increase SAE PID
refresh on CAN when enabled. This probe uses only five individually verified
generic Mode 01 PIDs. No DTC, OEM, actuator, control, or write request was sent.

Adapter: OBDLink SX, STN1130 v5.10.1, COM4 at 115200, Ford ISO 15765-4 CAN 11/500.
Ignition on, engine off. OBDWiz and FORScan were closed. UART echo was disabled
during the probe; all changes were restored afterward.

Commands sent for initial full-response discovery:
  STI
  ATE0
  STPRS
  STPO
  AT H1
  AT SH 7DF
  ATAT 1
  STPTO 102
  STPX D:010C050F110D, T:250

The uncapped STPX returned two ISO-TP frames from one ECU. Raw response:
  7E8 10 0C 41 0C 09 54 05 8C
  7E8 21 0F 63 11 1E 0D 00 00

Reassembled 12-byte OBD payload:
  41 0C 09 54 05 8C 0F 63 11 1E 0D 00

This contains five values in one Mode 01 response: RPM 597, coolant 100 °C,
intake air 59 °C, throttle 11.8%, and vehicle speed 0 km/h. The uncapped call took
309.42 ms because it waited through the specified response timeout.

Response-count check:
  STPX D:010C050F110D, R:1, T:250
returned only the first frame:
  7E8 10 0C 41 0C 09 71 05 89
Elapsed 30.92 ms. This is incomplete and must not be used for combined PID
responses. It confirms that the response count applies to CAN frames in this
multi-frame exchange.

Cleanup succeeded:
  STPPMC -> OK
  STPTO 102 -> OK
  ATAT 1 -> OK
  AT H0 -> OK
  AT SH 7DF -> OK
  ATE1 -> OK
  STPX D:010C, R:1, T:250 -> valid 41 0C 09 5C

The documented `R:2` follow-up returned the complete two-frame response. One raw
sample was:
  7E8 10 0C 41 0C 00 00 05 8C
  7E8 21 0F 67 11 2A 0D 00 00
The payload parser stripped ISO-TP frame markers and trailing pad to recover the
declared 12 bytes: `41 0C 00 00 05 8C 0F 67 11 2A 0D 00`.

COM4/115200, echo off, headers on, ATAT1, functional header 7DF:
  20 combined requests, R:2 T:250 -> 100/100 PID values, 80.51 values/s
  100 individual STPX requests (20 cycles x 5 PIDs) -> 100/100 values, 32.63/s

Physical request header `7E0` returned 100/100 in 78.74 values/s, effectively
the same rate as the functional `7DF` request. Timing samples for the combined
query, 20 requests each, were:
  ATAT1 T=250: 100/100 values, 75.44/s
  ATAT1 T=100: 100/100 values, 81.62/s
  ATAT2 T=100: 100/100 values, 81.42/s
  ATAT1 T=70: 100/100 values, 79.43/s

The timing differences are small and based on short samples; keep the safer
T=250 setting until longer tests confirm a repeatable advantage. ATAT2 did not
improve the measured rate. Every bench run restored ATAT1, timeout 102, headers
off, functional header 7DF, echo on; final RPM reads succeeded.

Six-PID boundary check:
  STPX D:010C050F110D04, R:3, T:250
returned the expected 14-byte response over three CAN frames:
  7E8 10 0E 41 0C 00 00 05 8C
  7E8 21 0F 67 11 2A 0D 00 04
  7E8 22 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
Reassembled payload: `41 0C 00 00 05 8C 0F 67 11 2A 0D 00 04 00`.
Twenty six-PID requests delivered 120/120 values at 63.60 values/s. The same
six PIDs requested individually delivered 120/120 at 33.18 values/s. The six-PID
group was slower than the five-PID group because it crossed into a third CAN
frame.

The FRPM's separate `STBC` batched-command parser was also checked with
`STBC 0`; this firmware returned `?`, so host-command batching is unavailable on
this SX as installed. This is distinct from OBDwiz's “Combine SAE PID Requests”
feature, which combines multiple SAE PIDs inside one standard Mode 01 request.

The 35 advertised public candidates were grouped by response width so each
payload is at most 13 bytes (two CAN frames, `R:2`):
  STPX D:01040506070809, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:010B0D0E0F1113, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:011C2E2F303345, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:014647494A4C, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:01030C1F21, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:012331323C, R:2, T:250
  STPX D:013D424344, R:2, T:250
The 35 candidates were individually verified earlier. The bench then ran these
seven groups for 10 cycles each, parsing each payload against requested PID order
and the PIDs seed's byte widths:
  70 grouped requests, 350/350 positive PID values, 4.299 s total
  Aggregate rate: 81.42 PID values/s
All seven groups returned complete positive responses from PCM `7E8`. Cleanup
restored ATAT1, timeout 102, headers off, functional header 7DF, and echo on;
final RPM verification succeeded. No DTC, OEM, actuator, control, or write
request was included.

All seven groups were then run for 10 cycles each using `R:2` and `T:250`:
  70 grouped requests, 350/350 positive PID values, 4.299 s total
  Aggregate rate: 81.42 PID values/s
Every response had the expected PID order and byte widths. Cleanup restored
ATAT1, timeout 102, headers off, header 7DF, and echo on; final RPM verification
succeeded.
